<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
    BlueROV2 Heavy - Blue Robotics observation-class ROV, 8 thruster configuration.

    Includable, parameterised robot definition. Use from a parent scenario file with:

        <include file="bluerov2_heavy.scn">
            <arg name="robot_name" value="BLUEROV2"/>
            <arg name="robot_position" value="0.0 0.0 2.0"/>
        </include>

    The parent file must define the material "Neutral". Frame convention is NED:
    X forward, Y starboard, Z down.

    ================================ SOURCES ================================
    Meshes from github.com/bvibhav/stonefish_bluerov2 (Apache-2.0), by a co-author of the
    Stonefish ML paper cited in the README. Apache-2.0 is one-way compatible with GPL-3.0.

    Physical parameters from the identified model of the BlueROV2 *Heavy*:
      Wu, C.-J. (2018), "6-DoF Modelling and Control of a Remotely Operated Vehicle",
      MSc thesis, Flinders University. Tables 5.1-5.3, p.48.

        m  = 11.5 kg          W = 112.8 N,  B = 114.8 N  ->  net +2.0 N
        r_g = [0, 0, 0.02] m  r_b = [0, 0, 0] m          ->  CG 20 mm below CB
        Ix = Iy = Iz = 0.16 kg m2
        Added mass   Xu' -5.5,  Yv' -12.7,  Zw' -14.57 kg
        Linear damp  Xu  -4.03, Yv  -6.22,  Zw  -5.18  Ns/m
        Quad damp    Xuu -18.18, Yvv -21.66, Zww -36.99 Ns2/m2

    ============================ BUOYANCY MODELLING ============================
    bluerov2_phy.obj encloses 19.54 L, but the real vehicle displaces only 11.70 L
    (= B/(rho*g) = 114.8/9810). The simplified hull is a closed shrink-wrap around what is
    really an open frame, so it "traps" water that in reality flows straight through.

    Therefore the hull carries shape, drag and collision with buoyant="false", and the
    displacement is set explicitly by the internal Displacement part, sized to 11.70 L and
    centred on the origin so that the centre of buoyancy sits at r_b = [0,0,0]. The 20 mm
    CG/CB separation that keeps the vehicle upright then comes from <cg> on the hull.

    ============================== DRAG MODELLING ==============================
    Stonefish does not take Fossen matrices. It integrates pressure and shear per mesh face
    (SolidEntity::ComputeHydrodynamicForcesSubmerged), which for a body in pure translation
    reduces to
        form drag       F = 0.5 * rho * Cd * A_proj * v^2
        skin friction   F =       rho * Cf * S_eff  * v
    where A_proj is the projected area of the physics mesh along the flow and S_eff is
    |sum(v_t * A)| / v over the whole wetted surface. Cd and Cf are the per-axis vectors below,
    collapsed to a scalar by blending along the direction of the resulting force.

    Both forms invert directly against Wu's identified damping, so the coefficients here are
    DERIVED, not fitted to a top speed:
        Cd_i = 2 * Xuu_i / (rho * A_proj_i)        Cf_i = Xu_i / (rho * S_eff_i)
    integrated over BOTH external parts (the hull frame and the Wings mount proxy):
        A_proj = 0.02185  0.19841  0.30817  m2
        S_eff  = 0.9674   0.6372   0.4176   m2
    One shared set would then be Cd = 1.664 / 0.218 / 0.240, Cf = 0.00417 / 0.00976 / 0.01240.
    Sway, heave and all three viscous axes use exactly that. Only SURGE Cd is split between the
    two parts, to place the centre of drag (see TRIM below), keeping the sum on Wu:
        0.5 * rho * (1.761 * 0.01945 + 0.875 * 0.00240) = 18.18 = Xuu
    Measured on-axis surge drag at 1.5 m/s is 46.95 N against Wu's 46.95 N, and the law is
    quadratic: drag / (A_eff * v^2) came out 1.000 and 0.995 at 1.83 and 0.94 m/s.

    ======================= TRIM UNDER SURGE (was an open issue) =======================
    The vectored thrust line (z = +0.0725) sits 5 mm ABOVE the CG (z = +0.0775), so at steady
    speed thrust and drag form a couple of (z_drag - 0.0725) * F. That couple does not depend on
    where the CG is, only on the gap between the two lines of action, so no amount of ballast or
    CG tuning touches it. Restoring stiffness is only B*BG = 114.8 * 0.020 = 2.30 Nm/rad, so at
    full speed each millimetre of that gap is worth roughly 2 degrees of pitch.

    The Wings proxy is the knob that places the combined centre of drag. The hull frame's own
    centre sits at z = +0.079, just below the thrust line; the Wings sit at z = 0, well above it.
    A surge Cd of 0.875 on the Wings averages the two onto the thrust line. That number is
    calibrated against measured trim, which is what the proxy is for - it is not a shape
    coefficient. It is at least ordered the right way physically: a thin slab edge-on should be
    less bluff than the frame (0.875 < 1.761).

    This block used to blame the projected-area centroid and propose rebalancing the frame and
    mount areas. That was wrong, and the vehicle settled about 9 degrees nose-down. Four separate
    defects were compounding, all now fixed:
      1. bluerov2_frame_phy.obj lost 73% of its frontal area to inverted face winding - see
         PHYSICS MESH below. This dominated everything else.
      2. viscous_drag was set to 0.1 * quadratic_drag by analogy with the library defaults and
         never derived. rho * Cf * S_eff * v with Cf = 0.062 and S_eff = 0.833 m2 is 77.5 N at
         1.5 m/s where Wu's linear damping wants 6 N - so skin friction, not form drag, was
         setting the top speed that the surge Cd was supposedly calibrated against. The two
         errors cancelled in the speed measurement but not in the pitching moment.
      3. SolidEntity.cpp computed form drag as |v|^3 * A * cos(a) instead of |v|^2 * A * cos(a).
         Any Cd was then valid at exactly one speed, and the surge/heave drag ratio - which sets
         the trim angle of attack - was distorted by the ratio of those speeds.
      4. CorrectHydrodynamicForces scaled the drag TORQUE by a coefficient blended from the
         torque's own direction rather than the force's. Under surge the force picks up Cd.x
         while the pitching moment it induces picks up Cd.y, so each part's effective centre of
         drag was rescaled by an arbitrary factor and the balance computed here was meaningless.
    Items 3 and 4 were fixed in Library/src/entities/SolidEntity.cpp.

    ================================ PHYSICS MESH ================================
    bluerov2_frame_phy.obj must contain ONLY triangles and quads. rapidobj::Triangulate, which
    GeometryFileUtil uses, inverts the winding of polygons with more than four vertices when the
    file carries no vertex normals. The four 24-gon enclosure end caps therefore all came out
    facing -X: sum(A*n) over the mesh was [-0.0283, 0, 0] instead of zero, the two forward-facing
    caps (0.0142 m2 of the 0.0194 m2 frontal area) were excluded from the pressure integral, and
    the drag centre swung wildly with the smallest change in angle of attack. The caps are now
    pre-triangulated in the asset. For any new physics mesh, check that sum(A*n) == 0.

    =============================== THRUSTERS ===============================
    FDThrust (Library/include/actuators/ActuatorDynamics.h) computes, at bollard (u = 0):
        thrust = rho * D^4 * kt * n^2,  n = setpoint / 2pi  [rev/s]
    Solving for the T200 datasheet with D = 0.076, n = 3600/60 = 60 rev/s, rho = 1000:
        kt_forward = 51.5 N / (1000 * 0.076^4 * 60^2) = 51.5 / 120.1 = 0.429
        kt_reverse = 40.2 N / 120.1                                  = 0.335
    Wu section 5.3 approximates each thruster at ~40 N max (K = diag[40...]); the reverse
    figure above agrees almost exactly, and the forward one brackets it.

    ============================ KNOWN LIMITATIONS ============================
    - Added mass is computed by Stonefish from a geometry fit and has NO XML hook, so Wu's
      values (-5.5, -12.7, -14.57 kg) cannot be set. It is whatever the hull fit gives.
    - Linear and angular damping likewise have no direct hook; only quadratic form drag
      (quadratic_drag) and skin friction (viscous_drag) are settable. Rotational drag is
      purely geometry-driven, so Kpp/Mqq/Nrr cannot be matched.
    - torque_coeff is ESTIMATED at 0.045 from the GIRONA500 kq/kt ratio; no T200 torque
      data was found. It largely cancels across the symmetric thruster layout.
    - kt is derived for fresh water, the datasheet condition. In seawater (1025 kg/m3) the
      model produces ~2.5% more thrust than the datasheet figure.
    - Thruster-thruster and thruster-hull interaction losses are NOT modelled: each unit makes
      its full datasheet bollard thrust, so the four vectored units sum to ~141 N where the real
      vehicle is quoted at 9 kgf = 88 N. Wu section 5.3 notes the same limitation. Drag is now
      pinned to Wu rather than to the published top speed, so this over-thrust is no longer
      absorbed anywhere and shows up directly: the model tops out at ~1.83 m/s against the
      published ~1.5 m/s. Closing that means correcting the thrust model, not the drag.
    - The physics hull is a closed shrink-wrap, so its plan area (0.308 m2) is 14x its frontal
      area (0.0219 m2) where the real vehicle is an open frame. The per-axis coefficients above
      correct this on-axis, but Stonefish blends them along the resultant force direction, so a
      few degrees of angle of attack still inflate surge drag well above Wu. At the trimmed
      1.4 deg the excess is about 29%.
    - The CG/CB datum ("vehicle centre" at z = +0.0575, used by <cg> and by the Displacement
      part) was taken from bluerov2_phy.obj, which is no longer in the repo. The physics mesh
      actually in use spans z -0.052..0.184, centre z = +0.066, and its enclosed volume centroid
      is z = +0.078. Re-datuming to either makes the trim markedly worse, so 0.0575 is kept, but
      it is an anchor to a deleted asset rather than a measurement.

    ============================= CAMERA AND LIGHTING =============================
    A vision sensor looks along the +Z axis of its own frame, with -Y as image up
    (Camera::UpdateTransform in Library/src/sensors/vision/Camera.cpp). That is a Z-forward
    optical convention sitting inside an X-forward NED body frame, so rpy="0 0 0" does NOT
    give a forward-looking camera - it gives one aimed straight DOWN and rolled 90 deg, which
    is what this file had while claiming otherwise. The same applies to <light>, whose beam
    axis is also its own +Z (Light::UpdateTransform).

    To aim a sensor forward and tilted down by an angle a, keeping image-right on starboard:
        rpy = (pi/2 - a, 0, pi/2)
    Rz(pi/2)*Rx(pi/2 - a) then has columns X = starboard (image right), Y = down-ish
    (so -Y is up), Z = forward, tilted down by a. The camera uses a = 0, the two Lumens
    a = 15 deg so they light the water the camera is looking into.

    The camera sits at x = 0.24, just clear of the bow: the visual hull reaches x = 0.222, and
    x = 0.22 as originally written put the viewpoint inside the dome. Its height z = 0 is the
    centreline of the upper electronics enclosure (the bow geometry splits into two tubes, at
    z = -0.04..0.05 and z = 0.10..0.14), which is where the real camera looks out.

    The picture is NOT displayed by this file. Colour cameras have no <display> element in
    ScenarioParser - the only route is Camera::setDisplayOnScreen from C++, which BlueROV2Test
    does, cycling the inset size on the V key.

    ============================== MEASURED BEHAVIOUR ==============================
    Full surge, free-running, fully submerged, still water:
        top speed                      1.83 m/s    (published ~1.5; see thruster note above)
        pitch                         -0.2 deg
        pitch at 60% surge            +2.3 deg
        pitch, full surge, depth held -1.1 deg
        sway drift                     0.000 m/s
        yaw drift                      under 1.5 deg over 50 s
        free rise, all stop            0.171 m/s   (Wu: Zw*v + Zww*v^2 = 2.00 N = net buoyancy)
        on-axis surge drag @ 1.5 m/s   46.95 N     (Wu: 46.95 N)
        thrust per unit                51.52 N bollard (T200 datasheet: 51.5 N)
-->
<scenario>
	<looks>
		<look name="brov_hull" gray="1.0" roughness="0.4" metalness="0.1" texture="bluerov2/br2.png"/>
		<look name="brov_wings" rgb="0.05 0.05 0.06" roughness="0.6" metalness="0.3"/>
		<look name="brov_hidden" gray="0.5" roughness="0.9"/>
		<look name="brov_propeller" gray="0.85" roughness="0.35"/>
	</looks>

	<robot name="$(arg robot_name)" fixed="false" self_collisions="false">
		<base_link name="Vehicle" type="compound" physics="submerged">
			<!-- Hull: shape, drag and collision. Carries the whole vehicle's mass properties.
			     buoyant="false" because the mesh over-encloses (see BUOYANCY MODELLING above). -->
			<external_part name="Hull" type="model" physics="submerged" buoyant="false">
				<physical>
					<mesh filename="bluerov2/bluerov2_frame_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
					<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
				</physical>
				<visual>
					<mesh filename="bluerov2/bluerov2.obj" scale="1.0"/>
					<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
				</visual>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<look name="brov_hull"/>
				<mass value="11.5"/>
				<inertia xyz="0.16 0.16 0.16"/>
				<cg xyz="-0.010 0.0 0.0775" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
				<hydrodynamics quadratic_drag="1.761 0.218 0.240" viscous_drag="0.00417 0.00976 0.01240"/>
				<compound_transform rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
			</external_part>

			<!-- Heavy outboard thruster mounts. The 85k-triangle visual mesh is far too heavy to
			     run per-face hydrodynamics on, so a small generated proxy carries the physics.
			     Its height and area matter: the mounts sit at the vertical thrusters' height
			     (z = 0), well above the vectored thrust line, so this part is what places the
			     vehicle's combined centre of drag - see TRIM UNDER SURGE in the header. Its surge
			     Cd (0.875) is calibrated against measured trim; the other five coefficients are
			     shared with the hull. The <hydrodynamics> block is NOT optional: a part without
			     one silently inherits an ellipsoid geometry fit, which for this thin slab gives
			     Cd ~ 0.04/0.025/1.0 and leaves it no authority over the trim at all. -->
			<external_part name="Wings" type="model" physics="submerged" buoyant="false">
				<physical>
					<mesh filename="bluerov2/bluerov2_wings_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
					<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
				</physical>
				<visual>
					<mesh filename="bluerov2/bluerov2_wings.obj" scale="1.0"/>
					<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
				</visual>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<look name="brov_wings"/>
				<mass value="0.001"/>
				<hydrodynamics quadratic_drag="0.875 0.218 0.240" viscous_drag="0.00417 0.00976 0.01240"/>
				<compound_transform rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
			</external_part>

			<!-- Displacement volume: 0.40 x 0.28 x 0.1045 = 11.704 L, centred on the MESH CENTROID
			     (-0.010, 0, 0.0575), not on the mesh origin: bluerov2_phy.obj spans Z -0.072..0.187,
			     so its origin sits about 58 mm above the vehicle centre. Wu's r_b/r_g are relative to
			     the vehicle centre, so both the CB here and the <cg> above are offset to match. Getting
			     this wrong puts the thrusters far below the CG and the vehicle pitches nose-up ~70 deg
			     under surge. CG is 20 mm below CB, per Wu r_g - r_b. -->
			<internal_part name="Displacement" type="box" physics="submerged" buoyant="true">
				<dimensions xyz="0.400 0.280 0.1045"/>
				<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<mass value="0.001"/>
				<look name="brov_hidden"/>
				<compound_transform rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="-0.010 0.0 0.0575"/>
			</internal_part>
		</base_link>

		<!-- ==== 8 x T200. Horizontal set vectored at +/-45 deg, vertical set outboard ====
		     Handedness of the vectored set is (cw, ccw, cw, ccw), i.e. each SIDE shares a
		     rotation direction. This is not cosmetic. Reaction torque acts along each thruster
		     axis with sign -handedness*sign(thrust), so under surge the alternative arrangement
		     (cw, ccw, ccw, cw) sums to (0, +2.83Q, 0) - a pure pitching moment that tipped the
		     vehicle nose-up ~20 deg. The arrangement below sums to zero.
		     cw.obj / ccw.obj match each thruster's handedness. -->

		<actuator name="ThrusterFrontRight" type="thruster">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 -0.7854" xyz="0.1355 0.1 0.0725"/>
			<specs max_setpoint="${3600.0/60.0*2.0*pi}" normalized_setpoint="true"/>
			<propeller diameter="0.076" right="true">
				<mesh filename="bluerov2/cw.obj" scale="1.0"/>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<look name="brov_propeller"/>
			</propeller>
			<rotor_dynamics type="mechanical_pi">
				<!-- J sets the rotor loop time constant tau = J/kp. Left to the mesh-derived value the
				     T200 propeller gives J so small that the discrete loop is unstable at dt = 5 ms
				     (it needs tau > dt/2). 0.05 gives tau = 50 ms, i.e. spin-up in ~0.15 s. -->
				<rotor_inertia value="0.05"/>
				<kp value="1.0"/>
				<ki value="0.5"/>
				<ilimit value="2.0"/>
			</rotor_dynamics>
			<thrust_model type="fluid_dynamics">
				<thrust_coeff forward="0.429" reverse="0.335"/>
				<torque_coeff value="0.045"/>
			</thrust_model>
		</actuator>

		<actuator name="ThrusterFrontLeft" type="thruster">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 0.7854" xyz="0.1355 -0.1 0.0725"/>
			<specs max_setpoint="${3600.0/60.0*2.0*pi}" normalized_setpoint="true"/>
			<propeller diameter="0.076" right="false">
				<mesh filename="bluerov2/ccw.obj" scale="1.0"/>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<look name="brov_propeller"/>
			</propeller>
			<rotor_dynamics type="mechanical_pi">
				<!-- J sets the rotor loop time constant tau = J/kp. Left to the mesh-derived value the
				     T200 propeller gives J so small that the discrete loop is unstable at dt = 5 ms
				     (it needs tau > dt/2). 0.05 gives tau = 50 ms, i.e. spin-up in ~0.15 s. -->
				<rotor_inertia value="0.05"/>
				<kp value="1.0"/>
				<ki value="0.5"/>
				<ilimit value="2.0"/>
			</rotor_dynamics>
			<thrust_model type="fluid_dynamics">
				<thrust_coeff forward="0.429" reverse="0.335"/>
				<torque_coeff value="0.045"/>
			</thrust_model>
		</actuator>

		<actuator name="ThrusterBackRight" type="thruster">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 -2.3562" xyz="-0.1475 0.1 0.0725"/>
			<specs max_setpoint="${3600.0/60.0*2.0*pi}" normalized_setpoint="true"/>
			<propeller diameter="0.076" right="true">
				<mesh filename="bluerov2/cw.obj" scale="1.0"/>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<look name="brov_propeller"/>
			</propeller>
			<rotor_dynamics type="mechanical_pi">
				<!-- J sets the rotor loop time constant tau = J/kp. Left to the mesh-derived value the
				     T200 propeller gives J so small that the discrete loop is unstable at dt = 5 ms
				     (it needs tau > dt/2). 0.05 gives tau = 50 ms, i.e. spin-up in ~0.15 s. -->
				<rotor_inertia value="0.05"/>
				<kp value="1.0"/>
				<ki value="0.5"/>
				<ilimit value="2.0"/>
			</rotor_dynamics>
			<thrust_model type="fluid_dynamics">
				<thrust_coeff forward="0.429" reverse="0.335"/>
				<torque_coeff value="0.045"/>
			</thrust_model>
		</actuator>

		<actuator name="ThrusterBackLeft" type="thruster">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 2.3562" xyz="-0.1475 -0.1 0.0725"/>
			<specs max_setpoint="${3600.0/60.0*2.0*pi}" normalized_setpoint="true"/>
			<propeller diameter="0.076" right="false">
				<mesh filename="bluerov2/ccw.obj" scale="1.0"/>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<look name="brov_propeller"/>
			</propeller>
			<rotor_dynamics type="mechanical_pi">
				<!-- J sets the rotor loop time constant tau = J/kp. Left to the mesh-derived value the
				     T200 propeller gives J so small that the discrete loop is unstable at dt = 5 ms
				     (it needs tau > dt/2). 0.05 gives tau = 50 ms, i.e. spin-up in ~0.15 s. -->
				<rotor_inertia value="0.05"/>
				<kp value="1.0"/>
				<ki value="0.5"/>
				<ilimit value="2.0"/>
			</rotor_dynamics>
			<thrust_model type="fluid_dynamics">
				<thrust_coeff forward="0.429" reverse="0.335"/>
				<torque_coeff value="0.045"/>
			</thrust_model>
		</actuator>

		<actuator name="ThrusterVertFrontRight" type="thruster">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 -1.5708 0.0" xyz="0.12 0.218 0.0"/>
			<specs max_setpoint="${3600.0/60.0*2.0*pi}" normalized_setpoint="true"/>
			<propeller diameter="0.076" right="true">
				<mesh filename="bluerov2/cw.obj" scale="1.0"/>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<look name="brov_propeller"/>
			</propeller>
			<rotor_dynamics type="mechanical_pi">
				<!-- J sets the rotor loop time constant tau = J/kp. Left to the mesh-derived value the
				     T200 propeller gives J so small that the discrete loop is unstable at dt = 5 ms
				     (it needs tau > dt/2). 0.05 gives tau = 50 ms, i.e. spin-up in ~0.15 s. -->
				<rotor_inertia value="0.05"/>
				<kp value="1.0"/>
				<ki value="0.5"/>
				<ilimit value="2.0"/>
			</rotor_dynamics>
			<thrust_model type="fluid_dynamics">
				<thrust_coeff forward="0.429" reverse="0.335"/>
				<torque_coeff value="0.045"/>
			</thrust_model>
		</actuator>

		<actuator name="ThrusterVertFrontLeft" type="thruster">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 -1.5708 0.0" xyz="0.12 -0.218 0.0"/>
			<specs max_setpoint="${3600.0/60.0*2.0*pi}" normalized_setpoint="true"/>
			<propeller diameter="0.076" right="false">
				<mesh filename="bluerov2/ccw.obj" scale="1.0"/>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<look name="brov_propeller"/>
			</propeller>
			<rotor_dynamics type="mechanical_pi">
				<!-- J sets the rotor loop time constant tau = J/kp. Left to the mesh-derived value the
				     T200 propeller gives J so small that the discrete loop is unstable at dt = 5 ms
				     (it needs tau > dt/2). 0.05 gives tau = 50 ms, i.e. spin-up in ~0.15 s. -->
				<rotor_inertia value="0.05"/>
				<kp value="1.0"/>
				<ki value="0.5"/>
				<ilimit value="2.0"/>
			</rotor_dynamics>
			<thrust_model type="fluid_dynamics">
				<thrust_coeff forward="0.429" reverse="0.335"/>
				<torque_coeff value="0.045"/>
			</thrust_model>
		</actuator>

		<actuator name="ThrusterVertBackRight" type="thruster">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 -1.5708 0.0" xyz="-0.12 0.218 0.0"/>
			<specs max_setpoint="${3600.0/60.0*2.0*pi}" normalized_setpoint="true"/>
			<propeller diameter="0.076" right="false">
				<mesh filename="bluerov2/ccw.obj" scale="1.0"/>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<look name="brov_propeller"/>
			</propeller>
			<rotor_dynamics type="mechanical_pi">
				<!-- J sets the rotor loop time constant tau = J/kp. Left to the mesh-derived value the
				     T200 propeller gives J so small that the discrete loop is unstable at dt = 5 ms
				     (it needs tau > dt/2). 0.05 gives tau = 50 ms, i.e. spin-up in ~0.15 s. -->
				<rotor_inertia value="0.05"/>
				<kp value="1.0"/>
				<ki value="0.5"/>
				<ilimit value="2.0"/>
			</rotor_dynamics>
			<thrust_model type="fluid_dynamics">
				<thrust_coeff forward="0.429" reverse="0.335"/>
				<torque_coeff value="0.045"/>
			</thrust_model>
		</actuator>

		<actuator name="ThrusterVertBackLeft" type="thruster">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 -1.5708 0.0" xyz="-0.12 -0.218 0.0"/>
			<specs max_setpoint="${3600.0/60.0*2.0*pi}" normalized_setpoint="true"/>
			<propeller diameter="0.076" right="true">
				<mesh filename="bluerov2/cw.obj" scale="1.0"/>
				<material name="Neutral"/>
				<look name="brov_propeller"/>
			</propeller>
			<rotor_dynamics type="mechanical_pi">
				<!-- J sets the rotor loop time constant tau = J/kp. Left to the mesh-derived value the
				     T200 propeller gives J so small that the discrete loop is unstable at dt = 5 ms
				     (it needs tau > dt/2). 0.05 gives tau = 50 ms, i.e. spin-up in ~0.15 s. -->
				<rotor_inertia value="0.05"/>
				<kp value="1.0"/>
				<ki value="0.5"/>
				<ilimit value="2.0"/>
			</rotor_dynamics>
			<thrust_model type="fluid_dynamics">
				<thrust_coeff forward="0.429" reverse="0.335"/>
				<torque_coeff value="0.045"/>
			</thrust_model>
		</actuator>

		<!-- ==== Sensors: a representative BlueROV2 research fit ==== -->
		<sensor name="odometry" type="odometry" rate="100.0">
			<history samples="1000"/>
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
			<noise position="0.02" velocity="0.01" angle="0.05" angular_velocity="0.05"/>
		</sensor>

		<sensor name="pressure" type="pressure" rate="10.0">
			<history samples="1"/>
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.10"/>
			<range pressure="1000000.0"/>
			<noise pressure="10.0"/>
		</sensor>

		<sensor name="imu" type="imu" rate="50.0">
			<history samples="1"/>
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
			<range angular_velocity="10.0 10.0 5.0" linear_acceleration="10.0"/>
			<noise angle="0.01 0.01 0.05" angular_velocity="0.05" yaw_drift="0.001" linear_acceleration="0.1"/>
		</sensor>

		<sensor name="dvl" type="dvl" rate="10.0">
			<history samples="1"/>
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="3.1416 0.0 -0.785" xyz="0.0 0.0 0.115"/>
			<specs beam_angle="30.0"/>
			<range velocity="5.0 5.0 5.0" altitude_min="0.3" altitude_max="50.0"/>
			<water_layer minimum_layer_size="2.0" boundary_near="5.0" boundary_far="20.0"/>
			<noise velocity="0.0015" altitude="0.001"/>
		</sensor>

		<sensor name="gps" type="gps" rate="1.0">
			<history samples="1"/>
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="0.0 0.0 -0.12"/>
			<noise ned_position="0.5"/>
		</sensor>

		<!-- Forward looking low-light camera, as on a standard BlueROV2. The rpy aims the
		     sensor's +Z view axis along body +X - see CAMERA AND LIGHTING in the header. -->
		<sensor name="camera" type="camera" rate="30.0">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<origin rpy="${pi/2} 0.0 ${pi/2}" xyz="0.24 0.0 0.0"/>
			<specs resolution_x="1280" resolution_y="720" horizontal_fov="80.0"/>
		</sensor>

		<!-- Lumen-style illumination, forward and slightly outboard, tilted 15 deg down.
		     A light emits along its own +Z, so this needs the same rpy treatment as the
		     camera; at rpy="0 0 0" both beams pointed straight at the seabed. -->
		<light name="LightPort">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<specs illuminance="15000.0" radius="0.02" cone_angle="90.0"/>
			<color temperature="5000.0"/>
			<origin rpy="${pi/2 - 0.2618} 0.0 ${pi/2}" xyz="0.20 -0.13 -0.04"/>
		</light>

		<light name="LightStarboard">
			<link name="Vehicle"/>
			<specs illuminance="15000.0" radius="0.02" cone_angle="90.0"/>
			<color temperature="5000.0"/>
			<origin rpy="${pi/2 - 0.2618} 0.0 ${pi/2}" xyz="0.20 0.13 -0.04"/>
		</light>

		<world_transform rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0" xyz="$(arg robot_position)"/>
	</robot>
</scenario>
